Compiling IVAN under different systems
--------------------------------------

CMake build (recommended)
-------------------------

Requirements:

   CMake 3.20 or newer
   SDL 1.2 development files (library + headers)
   any C++17 compiler (GCC, Clang, MSVC)

Development build -- builds straight into the build tree and runs
without installing:

   cmake --preset dev
   cmake --build --preset dev
   ./build/ivan

Release build installed into ./.local:

   cmake --preset local
   cmake --build --preset local
   cmake --install build-local

Without presets:

   cmake -S . -B build
   cmake --build build
   cmake --install build          # add --prefix to choose the prefix

Useful cache options (pass them to cmake -S/-B or with cmake-gui):

   -DIVAN_WIZARD=ON            enable wizard/cheat mode, as the old
                               Windows builds did (default OFF)
   -DIVAN_WILDERNESS_TEST=ON   build the headless wilderness diagnostic
                               (default OFF; POSIX only; see below)
   -DIVAN_INSTALL_PERMS=ON     old "make install" extras that need root:
                               chown/chgrp the highscore file, Bones/ and the
                               binary to the "games" group, and set the
                               setgid bit on the binary (the Bones/ directory
                               and the highscore file themselves are always
                               created on install)
   -DIVAN_DATADIR=DIR          directory whose ivan/ subdirectory holds
                               Script/ and Graphics/ (compiled in)
   -DIVAN_LOCALSTATEDIR=DIR    directory for the highscore file and
                               Bones/ (compiled in)
   -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=DIR  usual CMake install prefix

Note: DATADIR and LOCAL_STATE_DIR are baked into the binary, as they
always were, so they are taken from the configure step -- installing
with a different --prefix than the configured CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX
moves the files but not the paths the game looks for.

Wilderness diagnostic mode
--------------------------

Configure with -DIVAN_WILDERNESS_TEST=ON and run the binary with
`--wildernesstest [seeds]` (default 10, valid range 1..4096; anything
else is rejected before the run starts). It generates every wilderness
biome for that many deterministic seeds in one process and checks:

 - the encoded tile/slot boundaries, including that no tile maps onto
   the reserved world-map index, and that every slot it asks for is
   inside the container;
 - container capacity and profile validation: the shipped profile is
   accepted, a short container is rejected with a reason, a profile
   belonging to an original dungeon is rejected rather than silently
   agreed to, a container carrying another biome's profile is rejected
   too, and a per-level override that is not that container's biome is
   refused before a map is built; no original destination is claimed by
   a wilderness container and every other scripted destination still
   resolves;
 - per biome, per seed: the arrival square is the centre and is walkable
   (except on water), a walkable flood fill from it reaches the map
   edge, and a four-square-wide route (the same footprint-aware movement
   a large follower uses, not a literal straight band) reaches all four
   edges;
 - every creature standing on the map matches that biome's whitelist,
   resolved from the character's own configuration (class name plus
   adjective) rather than from a script array position, and every
   decoration carries a configuration that belongs to the biome;
 - vegetation, obstacle and water distribution: biomes whose generator
   always plants or builds are checked per seed, the stochastic ones
   are checked across the batch and required to reach several map
   cells, every ocean seed on its own must be mostly water, and the
   forests' decoration coverage must fall inside broad intended bands
   (roughly 13-35% for a jungle and 8-30% for the temperate forests)
   rather than collapse back to a few isolated groves, as a connected
   forest matrix also keeps a good fraction of the map in or next to
   vegetation;
 - the desert oasis, searched for separately across up to 64 seeds so
   that a single-seed run still covers it;
 - a save/load round trip of a level file: the serialized statistics
   come back exactly as written, the enchantment chances that
   level::Save() omits are re-derived from the script (checked on both a
   wilderness level and an original dungeon level whose script value is
   non-zero), and the occupants come back identical by id and position;
   a second load from the same file produces the same population, which
   is what "generate once, no respawn" means;
 - a complete game resume through the production game::Init() the
   "Continue Game" menu drives, run in its own process because Load()
   builds a new game instead of tearing an old one down: for both an
   ordinary save and the actual autosave prefix, the .sav and its level
   file are written, the resume reports success, the player is back in
   the same area with a mutation (money) and a dropped item intact, the
   level holds the same population, there are no duplicate entity ids
   and nothing enabled off the active area, an entity pool tick is safe,
   and leaving and re-entering the saved area brings back the same
   population; a save made while standing on the world map reloads
   through the .wm file the same way;
 - a refused first entry does not cost a map its one-time environment:
   each named destination (Attnam, New Attnam and the lair) is generated
   and refused before anybody can stand on it, then entered for real;
   the weather it was born with has to be there, both straight away and
   after the map is written out and reloaded, and after a complete
   save/load;
 - travel: a walking player is refused an all-water area, a companion
   that cannot stand on the destination refuses the whole transfer, both
   leaving the source area and every detached traveller exactly where
   they were; a companion that can follow is transferred with everybody
   on a square they can move on and that is free for their own
   footprint; a fully obstructed arrival square either finds another
   place to stand or refuses with the source untouched; an original
   dungeon and an original town transfer go through the same code path
   with the same placement checks; the `>` command itself (not merely
   the API it calls) enters a walkable biome tile and refuses an ocean
   one; and the sumo mirror's caller-supplied-player transfer works in
   both directions;
 - a multi-square follower: one that fits nowhere in a one-square-wide
   destination refuses the transfer, while a single-square one is
   accepted, so the whole footprint and not just the anchor square is
   what gets reserved;
 - a refused destination is released rather than parked: the container
   keeps no loaded level, the refused map has been written out, the
   enabled population across all teams is unchanged, an ordinary entity
   pool tick is safe with only the active area live, and re-entering
   reloads exactly what was written;
 - the global rain binding: it is installed when a freshly generated
   town is entered, it survives the destruction of a rainy source when
   that town is left, it comes back when the town is reloaded, and it is
   restored exactly when a newly generated or an already loaded
   destination is refused;
 - the world map: entering a biome tile from a synthetic world map, being
   refused an ocean tile, and walking back off the edge onto the very
   same world tile, with a follower that came along carried back in the
   world group; the return entries are carried by area serialization and
   survive the map being written out and read back;
 - the day/night cycle of an outdoor area, by driving the game tick
   through both halves and checking the sun and ambient luminance;
 - save cleanup: RemoveSaves() in both its death ("autosave only") and
   full forms never probes the reserved world-map slot, removes exactly
   the generated slots' files for the name it was given, and leaves the
   reserved name and an ungenerated slot's stray file alone;
 - an autosave is actually written, into the isolated directory;
 - the startup checks abort (exit status 4) for a reserved level slot,
   for a capacity mismatch, for a biome mismatch and for a per-level
   override that is not a wilderness profile, with a control case that
   must exit 0.

It exits non-zero if any check fails and needs no display, so it can be
scripted:

   cmake -S . -B build-wild -DIVAN_BACKEND=sdl3 -DIVAN_WILDERNESS_TEST=ON
   cmake --build build-wild -j
   SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy \
     timeout 1800 ./build-wild/ivan --wildernesstest 50

The run is bounded by the command's own timeout as well as by the seed
range, so a hang cannot be mistaken for a pass.

Everything the run writes goes into a fresh /tmp/ivan-wildtest-XXXXXX
directory. The working directory and $HOME are taken over before
ivanconfig::Initialize() and the rest of startup run, so no ordinary
configuration or save file is even read; a run that cannot establish
that environment stops instead of falling back on the real one. The
directory is deleted and checked gone before the result is printed, and
a run that is killed or crashes before that point leaves it behind for
inspection.

Because it drives the game through POSIX process, environment and file
APIs, `-DIVAN_WILDERNESS_TEST=ON` is only offered on POSIX platforms;
the option-off build is unaffected everywhere.

Not covered: the interactive menu layer -- picking an option from a
list, and cancelling the "do you want to leave?" prompt that the real
edge walk asks before it travels (the `>` command itself is driven
directly, and the world return uses the same production expression the
edge walk in char.cpp builds). Save cleanup is checked against isolated
files rather than through a full death-and-restart of a real character.
Bones never apply, because wilderness profiles do not enable them. Most
travel tests neutralise the player/monster team relation so that
placement and not line of sight decides the outcome; the "you can't
escape with hostiles nearby" rule has its own dedicated case, which
asserts the refusal and the restored source.

A refused transfer restores the source's occupants, their coordinates
and its rain binding. It does not undo side effects that already
happened before the refusal was known: a follower's voluntary action
may have been terminated and a "follows you" message may have been
shown. The transaction is about where everybody ends up, not about
replaying the turn.

`--wildernessabort <case>` and `--wildernessresume <phase> <name>` are
internal entry points used by the run above to exercise the abort and
save/resume cases in child processes; they are not meant to be called
directly.

Save compatibility: this change bumps SAVE_FILE_VERSION to 120 and
BONE_FILE_VERSION to 106. Level files gained one serialized field
(`level::FirstEntryInitDone`) and the world-map return entries are
carried once by the base area serialization. The version gate rejects a
mismatched .sav, an incompatible bone file is discarded and regenerated,
and a development build from before this change has to start a new game.
The version gate is not a comprehensive integrity check for the
subordinate level files.

A note on configuration ids: a script entry's `Config <number>` is an
explicit id read from the file (Script/char.dat and friends) and copied
into the database, while the position of that database inside
`GetConfigData()` is a separate, compact array offset that the parser
assigns as it reads. Preserving the script id keeps it a stable key;
the array offset shifts as soon as any earlier configuration is added
or removed and cannot be used as one. The biome population tables
therefore select by prototype name plus adjective, and the diagnostic
above reads the class name and adjective straight off the character's
own database rather than indexing `GetConfigData()` with an offset.
Using explicit ids plus validation instead of display strings is a
reasonable future cleanup, not a correctness requirement today.

--------------------------------------

Legacy DOS (DJGPP) build:

If you have DJGPP installed, type:

make -f ivandj.mak

--------------------------------------

The original GNU Autotools (./configure && make), MSVC 6.0 (.dsw/.dsp)
and MinGW (ivanmgw.mak) build files were removed in favor of CMake.
They can be restored from the git history.